# NODX Enterprise Handbook {#handbook}

This long-form document demonstrates a practical NODX source file with semantic
structure, readable fallbacks, agent-addressable nodes, and mixed rich content.
It is written for {{vars.company}} by {{vars.owner}}.

:::note {#executive-summary type="info"}
NODX is used here as an authoritative source format. Renderers may target web,
terminal, desktop, PDF, DOCX, or compact projections for agents without changing
the source document.
:::

::::section {#operating-model}
## Operating Model {#operating-model-heading}

The operating model separates **source**, *validation*, `canonicalization`, and
rendering. Agents should mutate stable node IDs such as @[decision-policy] and
must not rely on fragile text offsets.

:::quote {#principle-quote}
Readable source is the fallback. Semantic structure is the contract.
:::

### Review Checklist {#review-checklist}

- [x] Parse the source deterministically
- [x] Preserve unknown attributes and custom components
- [ ] Add package-level asset validation
- [ ] Add signature verification
- [ ] Publish browser and CLI renderers

### Rollout Phases {#rollout-phases}

1. Publish the reference parser and public corpus
2. Add independent parser parity checks
3. Add safe package reading
4. Add loss-aware converters

:::decision {#decision-policy approved="true"}
Approved policy nodes require an explicit agent policy override before mutation.
:::
::::

::::section {#risk}
## Risk Register {#risk-heading}

| Risk | Impact | Default Control |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Remote asset fetch | data exfiltration | offline by default |
| Unknown component | lost semantics | render fallback children |
| Ambiguous agent target | incorrect edit | fail closed |
| Untrusted SVG | active content | sanitize or fallback |

